# OOP

Write object-oriented Dart. Behavior lives on types, not loose maps, globals, or script-style functions.

- Encapsulate state. Dependents call methods/getters; they do not poke fields or parse maps.
- New work is a class (service, component, model, enum). Do not add a bag of top-level functions when a type already owns that job.
- GetX abstracts stay abstract: `Get.find` / `Foo.to` on the abstract; register the impl with `@GetPut.service(as: Abstract)`.
- Prefer composition over copying logic. Extend `CoreService` / `CoreComponent` / `CoreController` / `CoreView` instead of parallel hierarchies.
- Do not leak plugin types (`PackageInfo`, `Dio`, storage engines) past the owning service. Callers use this app's models.

```dart
// ❌ BAD — script + raw map
Map<String, String> deviceFacts() => {'os': Platform.operatingSystem};

// ✅ GOOD — type owns the data
class AppDeviceSnapshot {
  const AppDeviceSnapshot({required this.os});
  final String os;
}
```
